      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-43462",
      ":question/problem": "Express $$\n\\displaystyle 6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)=3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)=3\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right).\n$$"
    },
